===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|grc|πολυδάκτυλος}} {{suffix|en||-y}}, from {{affix|grc|πολυ-|δάκτυλος|t1=many|t2=fingers, toes|nocat=1}}, equivalent to {{af|en|poly-|dactyly}} or {{af|en|polydactyl|-y|id2=noun}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌpɒl.ɪˈdak.tɪl.i/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-polydactyly.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˌpɑl.iˈdæk.təl.i/|a=GA}}
* {{hyphenation|en|pol|y|dac|tyl|y}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|medicine|teratology}} A [[congenital]] [[condition]] in which a person or animal has more than the usual number of [[digit]]s (fingers or toes) on at least one of their hands or feet.
#* '''2003''', Karl-Heinz Grzeschik, ''66: Greig Cephalopolysyndactyly Syndrome'', Mary Ann McLaughlin (supervising editor), ''NORD Guide to Rare Disorders'', {{w|National Organization for Rare Disorders}}, {{w|Wolters Kluwer}} ({{w|Lippincott Williams & Wilkins}}), [https://books.google.com.au/books?id=99YPDvFWBB0C&pg=PA201&dq=%22polydactyly%22%7C%22polydactylies%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwi9t4TZgf6GAxUM4jgGHQLiAI4Q6AF6BAgvEAI#v=onepage&q=%22polydactyly%22%7C%22polydactylies%22&f=false page 201],
#*: Postaxial '''polydactyly''' is common in the hands and preaxial '''polydactyly''' in the feet.
#* {{quote-web|en|year=2008|work=Guinness World Records|section=Human Body — Extreme Bodies|url=http://www.guinnessworldrecords.com/records/human_body/extreme_bodies/most_fingers_and_toes_-_living_person.aspx|title=Most Fingers and Toes — Living Person|passage=Pranamya Menaria (India; born August 10, 2005) has 25 in total (12 fingers and 13 toes). This is as a result of the condition '''Polydactyly''' and Syndactyly.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2021|author=Leah W. Burke|chapter=3: Genetics of Associated Syndromes|editor=Donald R. Laub Jr.|title=Congenital Anomalies of the Upper Extremity|publisher=Springer|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=mQIcEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA42&dq=%22polydactyly%22%7C%22polydactylies%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwi9t4TZgf6GAxUM4jgGHQLiAI4Q6AF6BAhFEAI#v=onepage&q=%22polydactyly%22%7C%22polydactylies%22&f=false|page=42
|passage=The rare '''polydactylies''', that is, not clearly only postaxial or only preaxial, are the most likely to be associated with an underlying syndrome.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2023|author=Christianne van Nieuwenhoven; Steven Hovius|chapter=9: Thumb '''Polydactyly'''|editor=Giorgio Pajardi|title=Pediatric Hand Surgery|publisher=Springer|pageurl=https://books.google.com.au/books?id=M2HjEAAAQBAJ&pg=PA102&dq=%22polydactyly%22%7C%22polydactylies%22&hl=en&newbks=1&newbks_redir=0&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwi9t4TZgf6GAxUM4jgGHQLiAI4Q6AF6BAgkEAI#v=onepage&q=%22polydactyly%22%7C%22polydactylies%22&f=false|page=102
|passage='''Polydactyly''' with a [[triphalangeal]] thumb will be described in the chapter on the triphalangeal thumb.{{...}}The incidence of '''polydactyly''' depends on the population studied and the definition used. Region, ethnicity and combined numbers of all '''polydactylies''', or only radial or ulnar-sided '''polydactyly''', provide very different incidences in published series.}}

